# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: deferred-alfe-plugin-followups — what the first plug-in system left for later
#+STATUS: DEFERRED
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: feature
#+DEPENDS: alfe-plugin-epure-windows-validation
#+BRANCH: feature-alfe-plugin-followups

Split out of =alfe-plugin-model= when it closed (alfe 2.2.76).

* 1. A compile cache for source plug-ins

A source plug-in is compiled at every start (SBCL compiles each top-level
form of a =load=ed source). Small for EPURE and EPUREE, not free. Cache the
result in =$XDG_CACHE_HOME/clautolisp/plug-in/NAME.VERSION-IMPL.plugin=
keyed by the source's write date, best effort. Not done: an explicit
=--compile-plugin= plus =make install-plugins= covers the shipped ones, and a
cache is one more thing to invalidate.

* 2. What =load_epuree.lsp= does besides loading, and the installed bundle

=epuree.alpm= (pjb, 2026-09-21) lists the sources of the /source tree/ in load
order — =src/{core,comu}= — and REPLACES the two loaders, =load_epuree.lsp=
and =loader.lsp=, which it does not list (a loader can be generated from it:
=alpm-generate-loader=, checked). But =load_epuree.lsp= is not only a loader.
Four things in it are not loading and are therefore NOT done when EPUREE is
loaded through ALPM: the path-separator shims (=findfile=, =load=, =open=,
=vl-file-*= accept "\"), the Windows environment shim (=APPDATA=,
=USERPROFILE=, …), the bundle-root anchoring (=epuree:*bundle-root*=,
=EPUREE_ROOT=, =com_racdao=) and the support-path injection. =core.lsp= copes
without them (=*root*= / =*bundle-root*= default to "." and follow
=EPUREE_ROOT= / =EPUREE_BUNDLE_ROOT=), but EPUREE on macOS/Linux loses the
shims. Fix in the epuree repository: move those four out of =load_epuree.lsp=
into source files, list them in =epuree.alpm=, and let the build install the
=.alpm= with the bundle (=make install= -> =/opt/epuree/{load_epuree.lsp,lisp/,
ES/,COM/,…}=, whose bundle root =load_epuree.lsp= derives from where it sits).
About forty scripts and the build load =load_epuree.lsp=: it stays until the
generated loader replaces it.

* 3. Hook timing at the edges

- =:cli-parsed= sees the backend /requested/; an =$ALFE_BACKEND_OVERRIDE=
  applies only when the backend is resolved, so a plug-in that needs the real
  one uses =:backend-selected= (EPURE does).
- Errors before the run context exists (parse errors, =--cad= resolution)
  reach no plug-in's =:error= hook; the plug-ins are not known active yet.
- =--dry-run= runs =:backend-selected= with =:dry-run-p t=; a plug-in must not
  demand the CAD there.

* 4. API stability

A compiled plug-in is tied to one alfe version by its file name; a source
plug-in is not, and the hook signatures are alfe's to change. A
=:requires-alfe= version range in =define-plugin= would let a source plug-in
say what it needs, and =--list-plugins= flag the ones that will not work.

* 5. Windows

The XDG lookups on Windows (=%APPDATA%\clautolisp\plug-in=, no default
=$XDG_DATA_DIRS=), =;= path lists and the =cd DIR &&= form of
=--print-command= are written and reasoned about, not run on Windows.
